<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-4-sequences-series" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Sequences and Series</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> Does <m>.99999\dots = 1</m>? Perhaps you have even heard <m>1+2+3+\dots = -\frac{1}{12}</m>, but 
      is that actually true? In any case, how can we make sense of infinite sums in the first place? To begin answering 
      these questions, we need to develop a solid understanding of sequences and series. We will develop the notion of 
      convergence, and learn a variety of tests to determine whether a given infinite sequence or series converges or diverges.
      This will begin to answer the initial questions posed, but amazingly our insights will lead to the power series representation
      of functions. This is one of the most useful applications of this course, and it will give us insight into how calculators are able to
      compute stuff like <m>\sin(7^{\circ})</m> or <m>e^{2.27}</m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

